      "id": "smart-choices-ch-2",
      "order": 2,
      "chapterNumber": 2,
      "headline": "Problem: How to Frame Your Decision to Solve the Right Puzzle",
      "keyInsight": "The most dangerous trap in decision-making is solving the wrong problem brilliantly. When an elevator in an office tower runs slow, building managers immediately ask how to install a faster motor.\n\nThat question triggers months of noisy construction and massive expenses. But behavioral scientists framed the dilemma differently: the real issue wasn't elevator speed, it was tenant boredom while waiting.\n\nThe cheap, brilliant fix was simply hanging mirrors next to the doors! How you state a problem creates an invisible fence around your thoughts.\n\nIf you ask a cramped, narrow question, your brain only produces cramped, narrow answers. The trick is stepping back to examine who built the fence in the first place.",
      "content": "Picture looking through a camera with a tight zoom lens pointed at a brick wall. All you see is a dark red surface, and you struggle to guess where you are. But zoom out just three clicks, and suddenly you discover a sunny garden with an open gateway right next to you!\n\nMost people stay trapped because they accept the initial trigger of a decision without question. If your boss asks you to choose between two tedious assignments, you assume those are your only choices. You forget to ask whether the assignment itself needs doing at all.\n\nTo discover the true problem, you must become an investigative scientist. Ask yourself: Why am I facing this decision right now? What forced this crossroad onto my plate?\n\nWhen you challenge default assumptions, hidden paths reveal themselves. A family arguing over which expensive city apartment to buy might realize their real goal is shorter commute times. Suddenly, working remotely two days a week or moving near a train line solves the puzzle with half the stress.\n\nAlways draft your decision problem in several different ways before picking a direction. Reframe it from the perspective of an outsider, a customer, or a partner. By testing different window frames, you ensure you don't spend years climbing a ladder that leans against the wrong building.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Before rushing to find an answer, make sure you are solving the right problem.",
      "audioSnippetText": "Solving the wrong problem with high precision is the ultimate waste of energy. Learn how reframing your initial question reveals creative solutions hiding in plain sight.",
